WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.341
[MUSIC]

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:11.538
There's a large amount of working from
home goes on,

00:00:11.538 --> 00:00:16.581
at all levels of employment, from the call
center people that we were dealing with,

00:00:16.581 --> 00:00:20.930
who are reasonably lowly paid and not
terribly skilled.

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:24.760
Up through programmers regularly work from
home,

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:28.780
database administrators reg, regularly
work from home.

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:34.570
Several of the most prominent Valley firms
encourage managers to work from home.

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:39.798
And this is the first evidence we have
about the efficacy of that.

00:00:39.798 --> 00:00:43.119
[MUSIC]

00:00:43.119 --> 00:00:49.020
What we found was that working from home
turned out to be a win-win.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:53.010
The employees, the employees were happier
because they

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:55.510
weren't having to commute an hour and a
half a day.

00:00:56.650 --> 00:00:59.550
The firm saved on real estate.

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:04.180
And the employees worked more and worked
more efficiently.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:09.160
More because saving time on commuting,
they didn't have to

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:14.170
take time off to go to the dentist or take
the kids to the doctor.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:19.070
And more effectively because working at

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:21.870
home was quieter than working in a noisy
call center.

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:25.230
So they didn't have to ask clients to
repeat themselves.

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:30.392
The people who were working at home worked
about 9% longer, a lot,

00:01:30.392 --> 00:01:35.060
9% bigger share of their assigned eight
hour shift.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:38.150
They took smaller breaks and fewer hour,
time off.

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:42.809
And they also were about 4% more
effective.

00:01:42.809 --> 00:01:46.970
So 13% gain and output.

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:52.146
Then once people were allowed to decide
where they wanted to

00:01:52.146 --> 00:01:54.870
work it jumped to 22%.

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:58.970
And a large part of this was that some
people found though they

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:03.310
thought they wanted to work at home, they
were just too lonely.

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:04.600
They wanted to get back to the office.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:11.227
And so the people who chose to work from
home, knowing what they were getting into,

00:02:11.227 --> 00:02:14.332
were even more effective and more
productive.

00:02:14.332 --> 00:02:17.605
[MUSIC]

00:02:17.605 --> 00:02:22.030
Working from home works really well for
some people, that, we've proven that.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:25.630
In some people, in some companies, some
jobs.

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:30.970
I don't think it necessarily would work
well for

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:33.850
all people in all jobs at all times.

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:41.280
There's, the, the manager has to
understand the tasks that

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:46.450
are involved in the job, understand the
mechanisms that can be used to control and

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:50.250
motivate the employees who are working at
home.

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:54.060
And has to understand the, the social
implications.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:58.410
A lot of our employees at Sea Trip became
lonely and

00:02:58.410 --> 00:02:59.680
didn't want to work at home any more.

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:03.040
You'd worry that in another context,

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:08.390
people who were not in frequent contact
with one another would be less innovative.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:12.580
You really just have to think through the
individual situation.

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:15.820
And maybe run your own experiment.

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:23.510
What the CEO, Marissa Mayer at Yahoo's
doing, isn't really much

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:27.750
about working from home or not working
from home, it's about not working.

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:36.570
Yahoo has been a company in big trouble,
in, in, a death spiral for several years.

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:42.810
And as I learned while being interviewed
repeatedly about her,

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:44.580
her outlawing working from home.

00:03:45.710 --> 00:03:48.980
The company just wasn't working and people
weren't working.

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:52.310
People who were nominally working from
home were, in fact,

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:55.590
setting up their own companies on, on
Yahoo's time.

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:59.990
And the culture at Yahoo had become really
dysfunctional.

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:05.840
And what I see her doing is trying to
change the culture.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:11.570
To save the company and, and that's, this
is one element of it.

00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:14.530
She's got, you've got to do that face to
face.

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:17.043
You can't do that over, over email.

00:04:17.043 --> 00:04:20.386
[MUSIC]

00:04:20.386 --> 00:04:25.818
If workers want to, want to experiment
with working from home

00:04:25.818 --> 00:04:31.350
one of the things to do is to approach the
boss with, with some data.

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:36.690
And the old data is a start but

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:40.260
this new data is, is gold standard stuff.

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:45.770
How much you wanna rely on our work rather
than

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:51.320
other stuff may depend a lot on how much
your job looks like the call centers.

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:56.680
Where the bosses can be sure that you'll
be motivated to,

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:59.108
to work once you're home and

00:04:59.108 --> 00:05:04.282
not to sit around in your bathrobe and
play Angry Birds.

00:05:04.282 --> 00:05:07.511
[MUSIC]

00:05:07.511 --> 00:05:12.360
Now this is an area where there's been
really no scientific research at all.

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:17.400
There's surveys and there's case studies.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:23.310
But you never have any idea what's really
cause and effect in a situation like that.

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:28.330
Whereas here, because of the
randomization, we could pretty well ensure

00:05:28.330 --> 00:05:34.280
that that the changes we saw were driven
by the changes in the management practice.

00:05:35.390 --> 00:05:40.309
And any time you find management, good
evidence that management really works, for

00:05:40.309 --> 00:05:44.535
somebody who's spent 40 plus years
teaching management, that's,

00:05:44.535 --> 00:05:45.728
that's a good day.

00:05:45.728 --> 00:05:55.728
[MUSIC]

